panels. A few isolated cells inside the panels show through in the background colour, and I'll call those \"holes\".\n- Several **smaller hollow shapes** scattered elsewhere. Each is 2 smaller than a panel in each direction, and each has its own pattern of background-coloured holes.\n\n**What the output is.** The output is the panel block cropped out of the input. Each panel's holes are filled with the panel colour, and one of the loose shapes sits inside each panel, centred with a 1-cell margin. The shape's own holes show the panel colour.\n\n**Which shape goes into which panel.** I compared hole counts, counting holes as 4-connected background regions, separately in the panels and in the shapes:\n- Pair 1:\n  - The 1-panel has 1 hole and receives the 2-frame, whose interior is one 2×2 hole region, so 1 component.\n  - The 4-panel has 2 holes and receives the 3-shape, whose two holes touch only diagonally, so 2 components.\n- Pair 2:\n  - The panels have 4, 3, 2 and 1 holes (panels 4, 3, 8 and 1).\n  - The shapes have these numbers of hole components: 9 has 3, 7 has 2, 1 has 1, and 5 has 4.\n  - Every pairing in the output matches equal numbers.\n\nSo the panel holes are a **count** that tells you which shape belongs there.\n\n**On the test.** Panels 9, 2, 7, 3, 8 and 1 have 3, 0, 5, 1, 4 and 2 holes. Shapes 9, 6, 0, 1, 3 and 5 have 5, 0, 3, 1, 2 and 4 hole components. Each count from 0 to 5 appears exactly once on each side, so the matching is unique. Shape 6 is solid with zero holes, and it goes to panel 2, which has none.\n\n**Construction.**\n1. The panel block is the largest multicolour non-background component. Crop its bounding box.\n2. Split it into panels, one per same-colour component inside the block. Count each panel's hole components, then fill the holes.\n3. For each loose shape, count its hole components. Stamp it centred into the panel with the same count. Shape cells keep the shape colour and its holes take the panel colour.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n\n    def holes(sub):\n        return len(components(sub == bg))\n\n    objs = objects(g, bg=bg, multicolour=True)\n    block = max(objs, key=lambda o: o[\"size\"])\n    y0, x0, y1, x1 = block[\"box\"]\n    out = crop(g, y0, x0, y1, x1)\n    emit(out.copy(), \"Crop the block of panels out of the input.\")\n\n    # panels: same-colour components inside the block\n    panels = {}\n    for c in sorted(set(out.flatten()) - {bg}):\n        for comp in components(out == c):\n            b = bbox(comp)\n            sub = out[b[0]:b[2]+1, b[1]:b[3]+1]\n            n = holes(sub)\n            sub[sub == bg] = c\n            panels[n] = (c, b)\n    emit(out.copy(), \"Count each panel's holes, then fill them with the panel colour.\")\n\n    blockset = set(block[\"cells\"])\n    for o in objects(g, bg=bg, multicolour=True):\n        if set(o[\"cells\"]) & blockset:\n            continue\n        sh = o[\"grid\"]\n        n = holes(sh)\n        if n not in panels:\n            continue\n        c, (py0, px0, py1, px1) = panels[n]\n        ph, pw = py1 - py0 + 1, px1 - px0 + 1\n        h, w = sh.shape\n        oy, ox = py0 + (ph - h) // 2, px0 + (pw - w) // 2\n        for yy in range(h):\n            for xx in range(w):\n                out[oy+yy, ox+xx] = sh[yy, xx] if sh[yy, xx] != bg else c\n        emit(out.copy(), f\"Shape with {n} hole groups goes into the panel with {n} holes (colour {c}).\")\n\n    emit(out.copy(), \"Final grid.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"objects":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Which shape goes into which panel.** I compared hole counts, counting holes as 4-connected background regions, separately in the panels and in the shapes:"}],"panels":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- A **block of solid-coloured panels** packed edge to edge."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Pair 1 has a 1-panel and a 4-panel, each 6×6."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Pair 2 has a 2×2 arrangement of 8×8 panels."}],"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The panel block is the largest multicolour non-background component."}],"template":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Stamp it centred into the panel with the same count."}],"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"A few isolated cells inside the panels show through in the background colour, and I'll call those \"holes\"."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"ch is 2 smaller than a panel in each direction, and each has its own pattern of background-coloured holes."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Each panel's holes are filled with the panel colour, and one of the loose shapes sits inside each panel, centred with a 1-cell margin."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Which shape goes into which panel.** I compared hole counts, counting holes as 4-connected background regions, separately in the panels and in the shapes:"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"So the panel holes are a **count** that tells you which shape belongs there."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Each count from 0 to 5 appears exactly once on each side, so the matching is unique."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**What the output is.** The output is the panel block cropped out of the input."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Crop its bounding box."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Crop the block of panels out of the input."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"36723881676707e62d88525660098f8c5e5721188a5553c0eb1e27e3cd4e1505","task_sha256":"d617eadc949efe8d46e33dad5bbd99d8f810bc2826d2f2d58bed5815b401d463","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
